<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masalit people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Masalit are a people of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, speaking a language of the Maban family and living mainly as farmers. In the late 19th century they formed the sultanate of Dar Masalit, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-geneina">El Geneina</a>, which resisted both Darfur and the French before passing under British rule in Sudan in the 1920s. They suffered heavily in the Darfur conflicts after 2003.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masalit-people.jpg" alt="Masalit people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ermanarich · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Masalit.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Masalit are an ethnic group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">western Sudan</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>. Their own tradition traces their origins to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, and holds that they migrated through Chad before settling in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. Most live in and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-geneina">Geneina</a>, the capital of West Darfur, though a few thousand live in Al Qadarif State in eastern Sudan and in South Darfur. Ethnologue counted 462,000 Masalit speakers in 2011, of whom 350,000 lived in Sudan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before 1874 the Masalit were divided between several polities of the region, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Sultanate of Darfur</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> conquered the Sultanate of Darfur in 1874, Hajjam Hasab Allah united the Masalit into a sultanate. His rule was seen as oppressive, and Ismail Abdel Nebi ousted him in 1883. In 1895 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a> invaded, and the sultan escaped capture by placing himself under the protection of the sultan of Wadai.</p><p>The fall of the Mahdist state in 1898 did not bring peace. The restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a> under '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-dinar">Ali Dinar</a> claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over all the small frontier states, and in 1903 he defeated the Masalit and captured and executed their sultan. The sultan's brother, Taj al-Din Isma'il, then rallied the Masalit and drove out the Darfuri army. The peace treaty of 1908 left 'Ali Dinar in de jure control, but in practice it placed Taj al-Din at the head of a small Masalit state under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> of Darfur.</p><p>Masalit groups were among the rebels who fought the Sudanese government and the pro-government Janjaweed militia in the War in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> from 2003. Reprisals and ethnic cleansing led to an estimated 170,000 deaths over two years. In the civil war of 2023 the Rapid Support Forces, a successor to the Janjaweed, began a new campaign of ethnic cleansing in Darfur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Masalit speak the Masalit language, of the Maban group of the Nilo-Saharan family. It is most closely related to Marfa, Maba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karanga">Karanga</a>, and is written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script. Most Masalit are also bilingual in Arabic, except in the central area, where the vernacular is mainly spoken.</p><p>They are chiefly subsistence farmers, growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> and other crops further south. A typical dwelling is conical, built of wood and thatch. The Masalit are Muslim, and their religious practice incorporates traditional beliefs and customs; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> first appeared in the area around the 17th century with wandering Muslim mystics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Masalit people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dud Murra continued the struggle from the countryside, with support from the neighbouring <b>Masalit</b>, until his surrender in 1911, and the French abolished the sultanate altogether in 1912.</blockquote><a href="/empire/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Masalit</b> lived on the western fringe of the Darfur Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/dar-masalit">Dar Masalit</a></li></ul></section>
